#heading(level:2,"The physical controls")
#para([#text("The right-side power switch controls the computer’s power rail. The Apple logo on the front is a touch control for shortcuts. The rear source selector chooses the LCD input, while the rear chime dial chooses the startup sound.")])
#para([#text("Turn the side volume knob clockwise to raise normal system volume and counterclockwise to lower it. Pressing the knob does nothing; it has no mute.")])
#heading(level:2,"Ports, wireless links and adapters")
#para([#text("One rear USB-C port is available to the user. The Raspberry Pi’s own ports are inside the case and out of reach. Use the hub for several devices, or for devices with older USB plugs.")])
#rail-def("Bluetooth LE",[#text("Bluetooth Low Energy, the wireless link the converted keyboard uses.")],[#text("The keyboard uses Bluetooth LE and the mouse uses its USB wireless receiver, which is plugged into the Raspberry Pi inside the case and stays there. Wi-Fi and Bluetooth are handled by the operating system. External audio input uses a separate USB capture adapter and audio-loop helper, not the rear composite-video RCA jack.")])
#heading(level:2,"The converted Apple keyboard")
#para([#text("The vintage Apple keyboard has been converted to Bluetooth LE using Matt Chesters’ keyboard-conversion project. It has its own battery, a nice!view display, a volume dial on its left-hand edge, and a power switch and USB-C charging port on its right-hand edge. It communicates separately from the mouse’s USB wireless receiver. Appendix A and the credits identify the conversion and its source.")])
#para([#text("The keyboard has been painted, not retrobrighted. Old Apple keyboards yellow with age. Today they are often bleached back to their original color, a process called retrobrighting, but in the old days the fix was a coat of paint in the nearest matching color. That is the source of the slight orange-peel texture you can feel on the case.")])
#block(breakable:false,sticky:true)[
#para([#text("The nice!view display, above the number keys, shows four things. The battery level is the keyboard’s own charge. The Bluetooth status shows an X when the keyboard isn’t connected, as in the photograph, where the PiForma was switched off; once connected, it shows a wireless symbol. The Bluetooth selector shows which of five Bluetooth profiles is in use, and the PiForma is profile 2. The graph shows your typing speed in letters per minute, not words.")])
]
#wide-illustration("2-4", "niceview-labeled.png", "The nice!view display: battery level, Bluetooth status, Bluetooth selector and letters per minute.", width:366.000pt)
#para([#text("The keyboard’s small 100 mAh battery lasts a couple of weeks of being switched on with light use. When the battery level runs low, charge it from the USB-C port on the right-hand edge.")])
#para([#text("Pressing down on the display changes the Bluetooth profile, one step per press; press on its plastic surround rather than the screen. Leave it alone: there is no reason to press it unless you want the keyboard to talk to a different device, and it should stay on profile 2 for the PiForma. The 3D-printed key around the display is a little too wide for its opening, so it tends to stick down when pressed. If it does, wedge it back up.")])
#heading(level:2,"The matching mouse")
#para([#text("The matching mouse combines Bambu wireless mouse-kit electronics with a printed Retro Wireless Computer Mouse shell. The scroll wheel and axle were replaced after the original wheel broke during modification. A USB wireless receiver provides the connection to the computer.")])
#heading(level:2,"Bose speakers and stands")
#para([#text("The Bose speakers are an optional extra. They connect to the rear audio output socket. The PiForma’s internal speaker works without them. Place them on stable supports with cables clear of the computer and its stand.")])
#wide-illustration("2-6", "bose-technical.png", "A Bose RoomMate speaker on its stand. The pair are optional, and travel outside the bag.", width:200.000pt)
#heading(level:2,"The bag and its fitted interior")
#para([#text("Every PiForma deserves a good home. This one travels in a waxed canvas backpack, finished with Otter Wax, so it shrugs off the weather and only looks better with age. Colorful embroidery and a set of patches give it a personality all its own. We are known for minimalist design. Here, we went a little rogue.")])
#para([#text("Inside, smooth green velvet holds the computer as if the two were made for each other, because they were. A corrugated-plastic liner gives the bag its shape, a foam bottom cushions it, and alignment grooves set the PiForma in exactly the right place. A wireless charging transmitter is concealed under the lining, with its cable reaching the back pocket, so the computer charges while it rests.")])
#para([#text("The foam locates the computer as well as cushioning it. The packing sequence in Chapter 1 protects the screen and puts the keyboard in its intended side position. A computer that only roughly fits won't charge; it has to sit in the grooves.")])
#para([#text("It looks right on anyone, and especially right over a tapestry jacket between business trips. There is no room left for clothes. You won't miss them. You'll be the envy of the departure lounge.")])
#wide-illustration("2-7", "bag-portrait.png", "Waxed canvas, embroidered, and fully loaded. The strap loop runs through four grommets and the clasp holds it closed.", width:137.649pt)
